cation, antigen presentation, and cytokine release are highlighted, illustrating the roles of fibroblasts and niche environments in cell signaling.)/K 0/P 1352 0 R/Pg 100 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj1366 0 obj<</BBox 1367 0 R/Height 321.846/O/Layout/Width 453.543>>endobj1367 0 obj[71.0362 424.8 524.579 746.646]endobj1368 0 obj<</A 1369 0 R/ActualText(Diagram illustrating risk factors affecting the colon. The top row shows The Western DietŽ \(high fat and protein\), Genetic Susceptibility,Ž and Food AdditivesŽ \(alcohol, salt, sugar, tobacco\). The middle section shows the lumen and colon epithelium\ levels, with microbes like Faecalibacterium and Bifidobacterium in the lumen, contrasted with harmful elements like Clostridioides difficile and hydrogen sulfide. The bottom shows the lamina propria, featuring immune cells like B cells, macrophages, dend\ritic cells, T cells, neutrophils, and plasma cells.)/Alt(Diagram illustrating risk factors affecting the colon. The top row shows The Western DietŽ \(high fat and protein\), Genetic Susceptibility,Ž and Food AdditivesŽ \(alcohol, salt, sugar, tobacco\). The middle section shows the lumen and colon epithelium\ levels, with microbes like Faecalibacterium and Bifidobacterium in the lumen, contrasted with harmful elements like Clostridioides difficile and hydrogen sulfide. The bottom shows the lamina propria, featuring immune cells like B cells, macrophages, dend\ritic cells, T cells, neutrophils, and plasma cells.)/K 0/P 1352 0 R/Pg 38 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj1369 0 obj<</BBox 1370 0 R/Height 326.551/O/Layout/Width 467.716>>endobj1370 0 obj[63.8929 104.655 531.609 431.206]endobj1371 0 obj<</A 1372 0 R/ActualText(Diagram illustrating the interplay between gut microbiota, immune cells, and intestinal health. It shows various bacteria, cells like macrophages and T-cells, and factors such as cytokines and antibodies. Labels indicate processes like gut microbiota dysb\iosis, gut barrier dysfunction, and immune dysregulation linked to inflammatory bowel disease \(IBD\). The layout includes sections of the gut lumen, lamina propria, and a blood vessel, highlighting immune responses and cell interactions.)/Alt(Diagram illustrating the interplay between gut microbiota, immune cells, and intestinal health. It shows various bacteria, cells like macrophages and T-cells, and factors such as cytokines and antibodies. Labels indicate processes like gut microbiota dysb\iosis, gut barrier dysfunction, and immune dysregulation linked to inflammatory bowel disease \(IBD\). The layout includes sections of the gut lumen, lamina propria, and a blood vessel, highlighting immune responses and cell interactions.)/K 0/P 1352 0 R/Pg 20 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj1372 0 obj<</BBox 1373 0 R/Height 319.691/O/Layout/Width 467.716>>endobj1373 0 obj[63.8929 120.869 531.609 440.561]endobj1374 0 obj<</Author(Ruilong Kou)/CreationDate(D:20250920080144+08'00')/Keywords(inflammatory bowel disease \(IBD\); gut microenvironment; gut microbiota; metabolites; intestinal epithelial barrier; immune system)/ModDate(D:20250920080144+08'00')/Subject(Kou R, Guo Y, Qin Z, Xu X, Liu Y, Wei W, Chen Y, Jian Z and Lan B \(2025\) Systemic dysregulation of the gut microenvironment plays a pivotal role in the onset and progression of inflammatory bowel disease. Front. Immunol. 16:1661386. doi: 10.3389/fimmu.2\025.1661386)/Title(Systemic dysregulation of the gut microenvironment plays a pivotal role in the onset and progression of inflammatory bowel disease)>>endobjxref
